
# Task 3: Mathematical Functions and Vectorization

## 1. Objective

The aim is to use mathematical functions and NumPy vectorization to process network latency measurements.

## 2. Inputs

The program generates one million random network latency readings between 5 and 250 milliseconds.

## 3. Mathematical Operations

The program converts milliseconds into seconds by dividing by 1000. It then squares each value.

## 4. Original Output

The program produced the first five squared latency values:

[0.03787665, 0.01266192, 0.04637842, 0.03092504, 0.00078812]

The loop execution time was 0.566 seconds, while NumPy vectorization took 0.0098 seconds.

## 5. Modified Input

I reduced the number of latency readings from 1,000,000 to 100,000.

## 6. Observation

NumPy vectorization was approximately 57.8 times faster than the ordinary Python loop. This shows that vectorization is more efficient when processing large amounts of data.

## 7. Interpretation

NumPy vectorization allows mathematical operations to be performed on many values at once. It is generally faster than using ordinary Python loops for large arrays.

## 8. Related Real-World Problem

Internet service providers can use mathematical operations to analyse network latency and monitor network performance.

## 9. Assumptions

The latency readings are randomly generated and fall between 5 and 250 milliseconds.

## 10. New Input Data

The number of latency readings was reduced to 100,000 while keeping the same latency range.

## 11. Output

The program produces 100,000 squared latency values and measures the execution time.

## 12. Interpretation of the Modified Problem

The modified program processes fewer network readings. This demonstrates how changing input size affects processing time.
